      "text": "To solve the given expression \\(1 - 4 + 7 - 10 + 13 - \\cdots - 46 + 49 - 52 + 55\\), we need to observe the pattern in the sequence of terms. The sequence alternates between positive and negative terms.\n\nFirst, let's write out the first few terms to identify the pattern:\n\\[1 - 4 + 7 - 10 + 13 - \\cdots - 46 + 49 - 52 + 55\\]\n\nWe can group the terms in pairs:\n\\[(1 - 4) + (7 - 10) + (13 - 16) + \\cdots + (49 - 52) + 55\\]\n\nEach pair \\( (a_i - a_{i+1}) \\) can be simplified as:\n\\[a_i - a_{i+1} = (2i - 1) - (2(i+1) - 1) = 2i - 1 - 2i - 2 + 1 = -1\\]\n\nSo, each pair \\((2i - 1) - (2(i+1) - 1)\\) equals \\(-1\\). Now, we need to determine how many such pairs there are before we reach the last term.\n\nThe sequence starts at 1 and ends at 55. The sequence of the first terms is \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots, 55\\). This is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a = 1\\) and the common difference \\(d = 2\\).\n\nTo find the number of terms \\(n\\) in this sequence, we use the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n\\[a_n = a + (n-1)d\\]\nSetting \\(a_n = 55\\):\n\\[55 = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2\\]\n\\[55 = 1 + 2n - 2\\]\n\\[55 = 2n - 1\\]\n\\[56 = 2n\\]\n\\[n = 28\\]\n\nSo, there are 28 terms in the sequence. Since we have 28 terms and each pair consists of 2 terms, the number of pairs is:\n\\[\\frac{28}{2} = 14\\]\n\nEach pair sums to \\(-1\\), so the sum of 14 pairs is:\n\\[14 \\times (-1) = -14\\]\n\nFinally, we need to add the last term, which is 55. Therefore, the total sum of the expression is:\n\\[-14 + 55 = 41\\]\n\nThus, the value of the expression is:\n\\[\\boxed{41}\\]",
